Hashmap internally uses array to store data. The index in the array is determined by a hash function that takes the key as input and produces an integer hash code.
This hash code is then mapped to an index in the array using modulo operation with the size of the array.

1. multiple objects mapping to same location - collision
2. computing hash time
3. unique distribution of keys (different index for different keys)

Solutions for collision:
--------------------
1. Open Hashing / Separate Chaining- Use Linked List at each index

    Pros: Simple to implement, handles collisions well
    Cons: Can lead to increased memory usage since LL is used and slower lookups if many collisions occur

2. Closed Hashing / Open Addressing - Linear Probing

    All elements are stored inside the hash table itself — no external lists.
    When a collision occurs, a new slot is found using a probing method.
    index = (hash(key) + i) % table_size


    Pros: More memory efficient since it doesn't use additional data structures for collisions
    Cons: Can lead to clustering and performance degradation with high load factors

3. Double Hashing
Pros: Reduces clustering by using a second hash function to determine the probe step size
Cons: More complex to implement and requires careful selection of hash functions
4. Cuckoo Hashing
Pros: Guarantees constant time lookups and handles collisions effectively
Cons: More complex insertion process and may require rehashing of existing keys
5. Robin Hood Hashing
Pros: Balances the load across the hash table, leading to improved performance
Cons: More complex insertion and deletion processes
6. Hopscotch Hashing
Pros: Maintains good cache performance and allows for efficient lookups
Cons: More complex to implement and manage the neighborhood of entries
